\\section*\{Explicit parallelization of the Milnor sphere \\(M_\{2,-1\}\\)\}\
\
Take the classical Milnor sphere\
\\[\
\\Sigma:=M_\{2,-1\}.\
\\]\
Milnor describes the relevant \\(S^3\\)-bundles over \\(S^4\\) by the clutching maps\
\\[\
T_\{h,j\}(a)z=a^hza^j,\\qquad a\\in S^3,\\ z\\in\\mathbb H.\
\\]\
When \\(h+j=1\\), the total space is a homotopy sphere; for \\(h=2,\\ j=-1\\), the integer \\(k=h-j=3\\) satisfies \\(k^2\\not\\equiv1\\pmod7\\), so Milnor's theorem shows that the resulting sphere is exotic. \
(\\href\{https://homepages.math.uic.edu/~kauffman/Milnor.pdf\}\{homepages.math.uic.edu\}) \
This is also the Milnor presentation of the Gromoll--Meyer generator of \\(\\Theta _7\\cong\\mathbb Z/28\\). \
(\\href\{https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0606769v1\}\{arxiv.org\})\
\
The construction below is entirely explicit. Its key algebraic step is the reduction of the stable tangent transition to quaternionic conjugation by the commutator\
\\[\
[a,v^\{-1\}]=av^\{-1\}a^\{-1\}v,\
\\]\
followed by an elementary closed null-homotopy in \\(\\operatorname\{Sp\}(2)\\).\
\
\\section*\{1. The two Milnor charts\}\
\
Let\
\\[\
D^4=\\\{x\\in\\mathbb H:|x|\\leq1\\\}.\
\\]\
Use two copies \\(D^4_\\pm\\times S^3\\), with boundary identification\
\\[\
\\boxed\{\\quad\
(a,v)_+\\sim \\bigl(a,a^2va^\{-1\}\\bigr)_-,\
\\qquad a,v\\in S^3.\
\\quad\}                                                   \\tag\{1\}\
\\]\
\
Equivalently, in Milnor's stereographic coordinates,\
\\[\
(u,v)_+\\sim\
\\left(\
\\frac\{u\}\{|u|^2\},\
\\,\\frac\{u^2vu^\{-1\}\}\{|u|\}\
\\right)_-\
=\
\\left(\
\\frac\{u\}\{|u|^2\},\
\\,a^2va^\{-1\}\
\\right)_-,\
\\qquad a=\\frac\{u\}\{|u|\}.\
\\]\
\
Write\
\\[\
T(a)z=a^2za^\{-1\}.                                        \\tag\{2\}\
\\]\
\
We identify the base \\(S^4\\) with the unit sphere in\
\\(\\mathbb R\\oplus\\mathbb H\\). For \\(x=ra\\), \\(r=|x|\\), define the two hemisphere maps\
\\[\
\\iota_\\varepsilon(x)\
=\
\\left(\
\\varepsilon\\cos\\frac\{\\pi r\}\{2\},\
\\frac\{\\sin(\\pi r/2)\}\{r\}x\
\\right),\
\\qquad\
\\varepsilon=\
\\begin\{cases\}\
+1&\\text\{on \}D^4_+,\\\\\
-1&\\text\{on \}D^4_-.\
\\end\{cases\}                                               \\tag\{3\}\
\\]\
At \\(r=0\\), the second component is interpreted by its smooth limit. On \\(r=1\\),\
\\[\
\\iota_+(a)=\\iota_-(a)=(0,a).\
\\]\
\
\\section*\{2. An explicit connection\}\
\
Introduce the standard smooth step function\
\\[\
\\eta(t)=\
\\begin\{cases\}\
0,&t\\leq0,\\\\[2mm]\
e^\{-1/t\},&t>0,\
\\end\{cases\}\
\\qquad\
s_*(t)=\\frac\{\\eta(t)\}\{\\eta(t)+\\eta(1-t)\},\
\\]\
and put\
\\[\
\\chi(r)=s_*(4r-2),\\qquad \\kappa(r)=1-\\chi(r).              \\tag\{4\}\
\\]\
Thus\
\\[\
\\chi(r)=0\\quad(r\\leq\\tfrac12),\\qquad\
\\chi(r)=1\\quad(r\\geq\\tfrac34),\
\\]\
while \\(\\kappa\\) has the opposite values.\
\
Let \\(A_+=0\\). On the minus chart, for \\(x=ra\\neq0\\) and \\(X\\in T_xD^4\\cong\\mathbb H\\), set\
\\[\
\\dot a=\\frac\{X-\\langle X,a\\rangle a\}\{r\},\
\\qquad\
\\phi=\\dot a\\,a^\{-1\}\\in\\operatorname\{Im\}\\mathbb H,\
\\]\
where \\(\\langle p,q\\rangle=\\operatorname\{Re\}(p\\bar q)\\), and define\
\\[\
\\boxed\{\\quad\
A_-(X)z\
=\
-\\chi(r)\
\\left[\
\\bigl(\\phi+a\\phi a^\{-1\}\\bigr)z-z\\phi\
\\right].\
\\quad\}                                                    \\tag\{5\}\
\\]\
Set \\(A_-=0\\) at \\(x=0\\); it is already identically zero near that point.\
\
Indeed,\
\\[\
(dT)_a(\\dot a)\\,T(a)^\{-1\}z\
=\
\\bigl(\\phi+a\\phi a^\{-1\}\\bigr)z-z\\phi,                    \\tag\{6\}\
\\]\
so near the equator\
\\[\
A_-=-\\,dT\\,T^\{-1\}.\
\\]\
This is exactly the gauge-transformation rule corresponding to \\(z_-=T(a)z_+\\).\
\
\\section*\{3. An explicit stable chart frame\}\
\
A tangent vector in a product chart will be written\
\\[\
(X,Y)\\in T_xD^4\\oplus T_vS^3,\
\\qquad\
\\operatorname\{Re\}(\\bar vY)=0.\
\\]\
Adjoin a scalar \\(s\\in\\mathbb R\\), representing the extra trivial line.\
\
Define the connection-corrected vertical component\
\\[\
W_\\varepsilon=Y+A_\\varepsilon(X)v,\
\\qquad\
\\xi_\\varepsilon=v^\{-1\}W_\\varepsilon\\in\\operatorname\{Im\}\\mathbb H. \\tag\{7\}\
\\]\
\
Write\
\\[\
d\\iota_\\varepsilon(X)+s\\,\\iota_\\varepsilon(x)\
=\
(y_0,y)\\in\\mathbb R\\oplus\\mathbb H.\
\\]\
Define\
\\[\
\\boxed\{\\quad\
\\Theta_\\varepsilon(X,Y;s)\
=\
\\bigl(y,\\ y_0+\\xi_\\varepsilon\\bigr)\
\\in\\mathbb H\\oplus\\mathbb H.\
\\quad\}                                                    \\tag\{8\}\
\\]\
\
This identifies\
\\[\
T\\Sigma\\oplus\\varepsilon^1\
\\quad\\text\{locally with\}\\quad\
\\mathbb H^2\\cong\\mathbb R^8.\
\\]\
\
At the equator, if\
\\[\
v_-=a^2v_+a^\{-1\},\
\\]\
then the connection relation gives\
\\[\
W_-=T(a)W_+.\
\\]\
Consequently,\
\\[\
\\xi_-\
=\
v_-^\{-1\}T(a)(v_+\\xi_+)\
=\
a\\xi_+a^\{-1\}.\
\\]\
If\
\\[\
C_q(z)=qzq^\{-1\},\
\\]\
then\
\\[\
\\boxed\{\\quad\
\\Theta_-=(I_\{\\mathbb H\}\\oplus C_a)\\Theta_+.\
\\quad\}                                                    \\tag\{9\}\
\\]\
\
Now introduce the fiber-dependent orthogonal gauge\
\\[\
Q(v)(p,q)=\\bigl(p,vqv^\{-1\}\\bigr),\
\\qquad\
Q(v)=I_\{\\mathbb H\}\\oplus C_v,                            \\tag\{10\}\
\\]\
and define\
\\[\
\\widehat\\Theta_\\varepsilon=Q(v)^\{-1\}\\Theta_\\varepsilon.  \\tag\{11\}\
\\]\
\
Using \\(v_-=a^2v_+a^\{-1\}\\), we obtain\
\\[\
\\begin\{aligned\}\
\\widehat\\Theta_-\
&=\
Q(v_-)^\{-1\}(I\\oplus C_a)Q(v_+)\\widehat\\Theta_+\\\\\
&=\
\\left(\
I_\{\\mathbb H\}\\oplus\
C_\{v_-^\{-1\}av_+\}\
\\right)\\widehat\\Theta_+.\
\\end\{aligned\}\
\\]\
But\
\\[\
v_-^\{-1\}av_+\
=\
a\\,v_+^\{-1\}a^\{-1\}v_+\
=\
[a,v_+^\{-1\}].\
\\]\
Therefore the complete stable transition has been reduced to\
\\[\
\\boxed\{\\quad\
\\widehat\\Theta_-\
=\
\\left(I_\{\\mathbb H\}\\oplus C_\{[a,v^\{-1\}]\}\\right)\
\\widehat\\Theta_+.\
\\quad\}                                                    \\tag\{12\}\
\\]\
\
This is the crucial reduction.\
\
\\section*\{4. A closed null-homotopy of the commutator transition\}\
\
For \\(q\\in S^3\\), let\
\\[\
D(q)=\
\\begin\{pmatrix\}\
1&0\\\\\
0&q\
\\end\{pmatrix\}\
\\in\\operatorname\{Sp\}(2).\
\\]\
For \\(0\\leq t\\leq1\\), put\
\\[\
R_t=\
\\begin\{pmatrix\}\
\\cos(\\pi t/2)&-\\sin(\\pi t/2)\\\\\
\\sin(\\pi t/2)& \\cos(\\pi t/2)\
\\end\{pmatrix\}\\in\\operatorname\{Sp\}(2),                    \\tag\{13\}\
\\]\
and\
\\[\
B_t(b)=R_tD(b)R_t^\{-1\}.\
\\]\
Define\
\\[\
\\boxed\{\\quad\
H_t(a,b)\
=\
D(a)B_t(b)D(a)^\{-1\}B_t(b)^\{-1\}.\
\\quad\}                                                    \\tag\{14\}\
\\]\
\
At \\(t=0\\),\
\\[\
H_0(a,b)=D([a,b]).\
\\]\
At \\(t=1\\),\
\\[\
B_1(b)=\
\\begin\{pmatrix\}\
b&0\\\\\
0&1\
\\end\{pmatrix\},\
\\]\
which commutes with \\(D(a)\\); hence\
\\[\
H_1(a,b)=I.\
\\]\
\
Let\
\\[\
\\rho:\\operatorname\{Sp\}(2)\\longrightarrow SO(\\mathbb H^2)\
\\]\
be the standard real representation by left multiplication on quaternionic columns. Let\
\\[\
\\mathcal J(p,q)=(p,\\bar q).\
\\]\
This is an orthogonal involution of \\(\\mathbb H^2\\). Define\
\\[\
\\boxed\{\\quad\
K_t(a,b)\
=\
\\rho\\bigl(H_t(a,b)\\bigr)\\,\
\\mathcal J\\,\
\\rho\\bigl(H_t(a,b)\\bigr)\\,\
\\mathcal J.\
\\quad\}                                                    \\tag\{15\}\
\\]\
\
If \\(c=[a,b]\\), then\
\\[\
\\rho(D(c))=I_\{\\mathbb H\}\\oplus L_c,\
\\]\
and\
\\[\
\\mathcal J\\rho(D(c))\\mathcal J\
=\
I_\{\\mathbb H\}\\oplus R_\{\\bar c\}.\
\\]\
It follows that\
\\[\
K_0(a,b)\
=\
I_\{\\mathbb H\}\\oplus L_cR_\{\\bar c\}\
=\
I_\{\\mathbb H\}\\oplus C_c,                                 \\tag\{16\}\
\\]\
whereas\
\\[\
K_1(a,b)=I_8.                                             \\tag\{17\}\
\\]\
\
Thus \\(K_t\\) is a completely explicit smooth null-homotopy of the transition in (12).\
\
Extend the transition over the plus disk by\
\\[\
\\boxed\{\\quad\
E(x,v)=\
\\begin\{cases\}\
K_\{\\kappa(r)\}(a,v^\{-1\}),&x=ra,\\ r>0,\\\\[1mm]\
I_8,&x=0.\
\\end\{cases\}\
\\quad\}                                                    \\tag\{18\}\
\\]\
Because \\(\\kappa=1\\) near \\(r=0\\), this is identically \\(I_8\\) near the center and is smooth there. Because \\(\\kappa=0\\) near \\(r=1\\),\
\\[\
E(a,v)=I_\{\\mathbb H\}\\oplus C_\{[a,v^\{-1\}]\}\
\\]\
throughout an equatorial collar.\
\
We now have the global stable trivialization\
\\[\
\\boxed\{\
\\Psi=\
\\begin\{cases\}\
E(x,v)\\widehat\\Theta_+,&(x,v)\\in D^4_+\\times S^3,\\\\[1mm]\
\\widehat\\Theta_-,&(x,v)\\in D^4_-\\times S^3.\
\\end\{cases\}\}\
                                                               \\tag\{19\}\
\\]\
Equation (12) and the boundary value of \\(E\\) show that the two formulas agree exactly in the gluing collar.\
\
For complete concreteness, if\
\\[\
q=q_0+q_1i+q_2j+q_3k,\
\\]\
left multiplication is represented by\
\\[\
L_q=\
\\begin\{pmatrix\}\
q_0&-q_1&-q_2&-q_3\\\\\
q_1&q_0&-q_3&q_2\\\\\
q_2&q_3&q_0&-q_1\\\\\
q_3&-q_2&q_1&q_0\
\\end\{pmatrix\}.                                            \\tag\{20\}\
\\]\
Therefore, for a quaternionic matrix\
\\(\
M=(m_\{\\alpha\\beta\})\\in\\operatorname\{Sp\}(2),\
\\)\
the ordinary real \\(8\\times8\\) matrix \\(\\rho(M)\\) is obtained by replacing \\(m_\{\\alpha\\beta\}\\) with \\(L_\{m_\{\\alpha\\beta\}\}\\). Thus every matrix in (15)--(19) is an elementary real matrix.\
\
\\section*\{5. Removing the stable line with octonions\}\
\
Equip \\(T\\Sigma\\) with the connection metric\
\\[\
g\\bigl((X,Y),(X',Y')\\bigr)\
=\
\\left\\langle d\\iota_\\varepsilon(X),\
d\\iota_\\varepsilon(X')\\right\\rangle\
+\
\\langle\\xi_\\varepsilon,\\xi'_\\varepsilon\\rangle.          \\tag\{21\}\
\\]\
Together with the standard metric on the added line, \\(\\Theta_\\varepsilon\\), \\(Q\\), \\(E\\), and hence \\(\\Psi\\), are orthogonal maps.\
\
Identify\
\\[\
\\mathbb H^2\\cong\\mathbb O\
\\]\
using the Cayley--Dickson multiplication\
\\[\
(p,q)(r,s)\
=\
\\bigl(pr-\\bar s\\,q,\\ sp+q\\bar r\\bigr).                   \\tag\{22\}\
\\]\
Use the standard imaginary octonion basis\
\\[\
\\begin\{aligned\}\
e_1&=(i,0),&e_2&=(j,0),&e_3&=(k,0),\\\\\
e_4&=(0,1),&e_5&=(0,i),&e_6&=(0,j),&e_7&=(0,k).\
\\end\{aligned\}\
\\]\
\
Define the unit vector\
\\[\
n(p)=\\Psi_p(0,1)\\in S^7\\subset\\mathbb O.                 \\tag\{23\}\
\\]\
\
The desired seven vector fields are the unique fields\
\\[\
\\boxed\{\\quad\
\\mathcal X_\\alpha(p)\\in T_p\\Sigma\
\\quad\\text\{satisfying\}\\quad\
\\Psi_p\\bigl(\\mathcal X_\\alpha(p),0\\bigr)\
=\
n(p)e_\\alpha,\
\\quad \\alpha=1,\\ldots,7.\
\\quad\}                                                    \\tag\{24\}\
\\]\
\
Equivalently,\
\\[\
\\boxed\{\\quad\
\\mathcal X_\\alpha(p)\
=\
\\operatorname\{pr\}_\{T\\Sigma\}\
\\Psi_p^\{-1\}\\bigl(n(p)e_\\alpha\\bigr).\
\\quad\}                                                    \\tag\{25\}\
\\]\
\
No projection is actually necessary: since left multiplication by the unit octonion \\(n(p)\\) is orthogonal,\
\\[\
n(p)e_\\alpha\\perp n(p)=\\Psi_p(0,1).\
\\]\
Because \\(\\Psi_p\\) is orthogonal, its inverse therefore has zero component in the added trivial line.\
\
If \\(n=(p,q)\\in\\mathbb H^2\\), the seven right-hand sides in (24) are explicitly\
\\[\
\\begin\{aligned\}\
ne_1&=(pi,-qi),&\
ne_2&=(pj,-qj),&\
ne_3&=(pk,-qk),\\\\\
ne_4&=(-q,p),&\
ne_5&=(iq,ip),&\
ne_6&=(jq,jp),&\
ne_7&=(kq,kp).\
\\end\{aligned\}                                             \\tag\{26\}\
\\]\
\
Since\
\\[\
\\\{n,ne_1,\\ldots,ne_7\\\}\
\\]\
is an orthonormal basis of \\(\\mathbb O\\), the fields\
\\[\
\\mathcal X_1,\\ldots,\\mathcal X_7\
\\]\
are pointwise orthonormal and hence form a parallelization.\
\
\\section*\{6. Fully expanded chart formula for the seven fields\}\
\
The formula (24) can be evaluated without any implicit bundle operation.\
\
Let\
\\[\
\\theta=\\frac\{\\pi r\}\{2\},\\qquad\
s_r=\\sin\\theta,\\qquad c_r=\\cos\\theta,\
\\]\
and set\
\\[\
G_+(x,v)=E(x,v),\\qquad G_-(x,v)=I_8.\
\\]\
From (19) and (23),\
\\[\
\\boxed\{\\quad\
n_\\varepsilon(x,v)\
=\
G_\\varepsilon(x,v)\
\\bigl(s_ra,\\ \\varepsilon c_r\\bigr)\
\\in\\mathbb H^2.\
\\quad\}                                                    \\tag\{27\}\
\\]\
Here \\(\\varepsilon c_r\\) is regarded as a real quaternion.\
\
For a fixed \\(\\alpha\\), compute\
\\[\
\\omega_\{\\alpha,\\varepsilon\}\
=\
n_\\varepsilon e_\\alpha\
\\]\
using (26), and then set\
\\[\
(z_1,z_2)\
=\
Q(v)\\,\
G_\\varepsilon(x,v)^\{-1\}\
\\omega_\{\\alpha,\\varepsilon\}.                             \\tag\{28\}\
\\]\
\
Put\
\\[\
\\lambda\
=\
\\varepsilon c_r\\,\\operatorname\{Re\}z_2\
+\
s_r\\langle z_1,a\\rangle,                                 \\tag\{29\}\
\\]\
and\
\\[\
t=\\operatorname\{Re\}z_2-\\lambda\\varepsilon c_r,\
\\qquad\
q=z_1-\\lambda s_ra,\
\\qquad\
\\xi=\\operatorname\{Im\}z_2.                                \\tag\{30\}\
\\]\
For the vectors \\(\\omega_\{\\alpha,\\varepsilon\}=n_\\varepsilon e_\\alpha\\), one has identically \\(\\lambda=0\\); retaining it makes the inverse formula valid for any target vector.\
\
The inverse of \\(d\\iota_\\varepsilon\\) is\
\\[\
\\boxed\{\\quad\
\\dot x\
=\
\\frac\{2\}\{\\pi\}\
\\left(\
-\\varepsilon s_r\\,t\
+\
c_r\\langle q,a\\rangle\
\\right)a\
+\
\\frac\{r\}\{s_r\}\
\\left(\
q-\\langle q,a\\rangle a\
\\right).\
\\quad\}                                                    \\tag\{31\}\
\\]\
At \\(r=0\\), this has the smooth value\
\\[\
\\dot x=\\frac\{2\}\{\\pi\}q.\
\\]\
\
Finally,\
\\[\
\\boxed\{\\quad\
\\dot v\
=\
v\\xi-A_\\varepsilon(\\dot x)v.\
\\quad\}                                                    \\tag\{32\}\
\\]\
\
Thus, in the product chart \\(D^4_\\varepsilon\\times S^3\\),\
\\[\
\\boxed\{\\quad\
\\mathcal X_\\alpha(x,v)\
=\
\\bigl(\\dot x,\\dot v\\bigr),\
\\quad \\alpha=1,\\ldots,7,\
\\quad\}                                                    \\tag\{33\}\
\\]\
where \\(\\dot x,\\dot v\\) are obtained successively from (27)--(32).\
\
These are literal quaternionic coordinate formulas for the seven vector fields.\
\
\\section*\{Why the formulas are globally smooth\}\
\
Near the center of the plus disk, \\(E=I_8\\), so no expression involving \\(a=x/|x|\\) remains. Near the center of the minus disk, \\(A_-=0\\). The functions \\(\\sin(\\pi r/2)/r\\) and \\(r/\\sin(\\pi r/2)\\) have smooth even extensions at \\(r=0\\). In an equatorial collar, \\(A_-=-dT\\,T^\{-1\}\\) and \\(E=K_0\\), so all transition identities hold not merely at the boundary but on an open collar. Consequently every derivative also matches across the gluing.\
\
Therefore (33) defines seven globally smooth, everywhere linearly independent vector fields on the exotic Milnor sphere \\(M_\{2,-1\}\\). The construction gives the requested explicit parallelization.\
